102 — Providers & Local Resources
Learn how Terraform providers work and create your first resources using the local provider to manage files on your machine.

Create a provider configuration
Create a main.tf file that configures the local provider. The local provider lets us manage files on our machine.
cat > main.tf << 'EOF'
terraform {
  required_providers {
    local = {
      source  = "hashicorp/local"
      version = "~> 2.4"
    }
  }
}

provider "local" {
  # No configuration needed for the local provider
}
EOF

What This Does
The `terraform` block declares required providers with their source and version. The `provider` block configures the provider. The local provider requires no configuration since it operates on your local filesystem.

Run terraform init to download the local provider plugin.

Commands to Run
terraform init

What This Does
Terraform init reads your configuration, identifies required providers, and downloads them to the `.terraform` directory. It also creates a lock file to ensure consistent provider versions.

Add a local_file resource
Add a resource block to create a file on your local filesystem.

Commands to Run
cat >> main.tf << 'EOF'

resource "local_file" "hello" {
  filename = "${path.module}/hello.txt"
  content  = "Hello from Terraform!\nThis file was created by Infrastructure as Code."
}
EOF

What This Does
The `resource` block defines infrastructure to manage. The syntax is `resource "<type>" "<name>"`. Here we're creating a `local_file` named `hello`. The `path.module` variable references the current directory.

Expected Outcome
The main.tf file now contains both the provider and resource configuration.

Examine the state file
Look at how Terraform tracks the resources it manages.

Commands to Run
cat terraform.tfstate

What This Does
The state file is JSON that records what Terraform has created. It maps your configuration to real resources. Never edit this file manually - let Terraform manage it.

Expected Outcome
A JSON file showing the local_file resource with its attributes like filename, content, and a unique ID.

Pro Tips
1. State files can contain sensitive data - don't commit them to Git
2. In production, use remote state backends (covered in later lessons)
Common Mistakes to Avoid
⚠️
Manually editing terraform.tfstate will corrupt Terraform's understanding of your infrastructure

